@meta
id: 08-12
title: Drill — swap the line below
chapter: 08
type: drill
skills: delete-d, put-p
par: 18
minutes: 5
reps: 10
vscode: Alt-Down, three times
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
The import convention here is local first, then infrastructure. Each pair
is the wrong way round. Swap all three.

One three-key move, three times. Nothing to work out.

@keys
| `dd` | cut the line |
| `p` | put it back below the cursor line |
| `j` | down a line |

@hints
`dd` then `p` on the same spot puts the line back one lower — which is
the same as swapping it with the line underneath.
--
After the swap the cursor is on the line you moved, so `j` puts you on
the top of the next pair.

@solution
    ddp  j ddp  j ddp  :wq<Enter>

`ddp` is the move-this-line-down key that vim never gave a key to. It
comes up constantly: reordering imports, pushing a log line past the
thing it logs, getting a guard clause above the work it guards.

Its mirror is `ddkP`, which moves a line up. Both are worth drilling
until they are one gesture rather than three keys, because reordering
lines is a thing you do dozens of times a day and it is where a mouse
user loses the most time.

The count works here too: `3dd` then `p` moves three lines down past one.
Be careful that you delete before you move — the register only holds the
last thing you took.

@start order.provider.ts
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';
import { QueueService } from '../../infrastructure/services/queue.service';
import { RefundRepository } from './refund.repository';

